To check whether the converted muscles pulled as strongly as their OpenSim counterparts, I compared their forces at 121 poses from a reference walk using the same joint positions and muscle activations. The reference forces and activations came from OpenSim static optimization, which estimates how muscles share the load at each pose \citep{opensim_static_optimization_docs}. For eight of twelve right-side muscles, MuJoCo reproduced the variation in force across poses and the mean force agreed within \qty{10}{\percent}. Two muscles illustrate why both checks matter. For the rectus femoris muscle, the mean forces were close, about \qty{48}{
ewton} in OpenSim and \qty{52}{
ewton} in MuJoCo. At one pose, however, OpenSim estimated \qty{96}{
ewton} and MuJoCo estimated \qty{416}{
ewton}. For the short head of the biceps femoris muscle, the forces rose and fell in a similar pattern, but MuJoCo's mean was about \qty{85}{\percent} higher. For both muscles, using the mean OpenSim force at every pose gave a smaller squared error than using the converted model's forces. These two muscles therefore require a separate check before using their forces to interpret a controller test. Appendix~ef{sec:muscle-models-compare} gives the calculation and all twelve results.

